A Kubernetes issue I faced recently: too many pods on one node

Recently, we noticed that multiple pods were getting scheduled on a node but were stuck in the 𝗶𝗻𝗶𝘁 state and not moving to running.

At first, it looked confusing because the pods were already assigned to nodes, yet they were not starting properly.

On digging deeper, we found that some nodes were getting a very high number of pods (50+). While this looked acceptable on paper, in practice the node did not have enough resources to reliably run that many pods along with system components and background workloads.

As a result, pods were scheduled but the node struggled to initialize and run them properly.

𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝘄𝗲 𝗱𝗶𝗱 𝗮𝘀 𝗮𝗻 𝗶𝗺𝗺𝗲𝗱𝗶𝗮𝘁𝗲 𝗳𝗶𝘅:

 • Reduced the maximum number of pods allowed per node

 • Set a safer pod limit so that a single node doesn’t get overloaded

 • Since we use autoscaling, new nodes were automatically launched to handle the remaining pods

After this change, pod initialization stabilized and workloads started behaving as expected.

This incident reinforced an important lesson for me:
pod density matters just as much as CPU and memory requests.

We’re now evaluating better long-term approaches to handle scaling without overloading individual nodes.
